{{also|sunni|Suni}}
==English==
{{wikipedia|Sunni Islam}}

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|ar|سُنِّيّ}}, from {{m|ar|سُنَّة|t=[[Sunna]]}} + {{m|ar|ـِيّ}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈsʊni/}}
* {{rhymes|en|ʊni}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Belonging or relating to the branch of [[Islam]] based on the [[Qur'an]], the [[Kutub al-Sittah]] (the [[hadith]]s which record the [[Sunna|Sunnah]]) and that places emphasis on the [[Sahabi|Sahabah]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=H. E. Wingate|year=1919|title=Records of Iraq, 1914-1966|volume=2|page=181|books.google.com/books?isbn=1852078200|editor=Alan de Lacy Rush, Jane Priestland year=2001|passage=I therefore strongly advocate the formation of a local capital but not at Hillah, which is too '''Sunni''' and near Baghdad}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Discourse and the Construction of Society|page=36|books.google.com/books?isbn=0195079094|author=Bruce Lincoln|year=1992|passage=members {{...}} came to view themselves collectively as the righteous descendants of Husayn confronting an evil and fundamentally alien ruler: a shah more Zoroastrian than Muslim, more '''Sunni''' than Shi'i, more Arab than Iranian, more Yazid than Husayn.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=The Making of Terrorism in Pakistan: Historical and social roots ...|page=98|books.google.com/books?isbn=1135169411|author=Eamon Murphy|year=2012|passage=Many Shias who had become more '''Sunni''' in their religious practices reverted back to their original sect.}}
#: {{coordinate terms|en|Shia}}

